```

### Why does the greedy approach work?

Spending removals on the smallest surplus counts first maximizes how many numbers we can fully reduce to a single copy. A number with frequency 2 needs only 1 removal to become distinct, while a number with frequency 5 needs 4 removals. By processing the cheapest conversions first, we convert the most numbers within our budget.

This is a classic greedy argument: per unit of budget spent, clearing a smaller surplus produces a higher return (one new distinct element for fewer removals).

### What happens when budget remains after clearing all duplicates?

If after clearing all duplicates we still have removals left, each additional removal must target an element that is already appearing exactly once. Each such removal destroys one distinct element. So we subtract the remaining budget from the distinct count.

```java
// After phase 1: all duplicates cleared, k = 2 remaining
// We have 7 distinct elements, but must remove 2 more
// distinct -= 2 -> 5 distinct elements
```

### What are the edge cases?

Several edge cases deserve attention.

- **K is zero**: no removals allowed, distinct count is just the number of unique values.
- **K >= array length**: can remove everything, result is 0.
- **All elements identical**: one distinct value, need count-1 removals to keep it.
- **All elements already distinct**: no duplicates to clear, budget removes distinct elements.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MaxDistinctEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // All identical
        System.out.println(maxDistinct(new int[]{5, 5, 5, 5}, 2)); // 1

        // All distinct, budget removes some
        System.out.println(maxDistinct(new int[]{1, 2, 3, 4}, 2)); // 2

        // K is zero
        System.out.println(maxDistinct(new int[]{1, 1, 2, 3}, 0)); // 3
    }

    static int maxDistinct(int[] arr, int k) {
        java.util.Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new java.util.HashMap<>();
        for (int n : arr) freq.merge(n, 1, Integer::sum);
        int distinct = 0;
        java.util.PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new java.util.PriorityQueue<>();
        for (int c : freq.values()) { if (c == 1) distinct++; else heap.offer(c); }
        while (!heap.isEmpty() && k > 0) {
            int c = heap.poll(); int need = c - 1;
            if (k >= need) { k -= need; distinct++; }
        }
        distinct -= k;
        return Math.max(0, distinct);
    }
}
```

